# Overview
#
# Passive voice makes the thing acted on the subject of the sentence
# (`the job is retried`). The sentence names the actor after `by` or not
# at all (`the file is read by the parser`). Grammar calls the actor
# that a passive sentence names after `by` the agent.
#
# This tier reports a passive with no modal verb before it. A modal verb
# is a helping verb that marks possibility, ability, or obligation, such
# as `can`, `might`, or `must`. A passive with no agent leaves the
# reader to work out who acts. The fix names the actor when the reader
# needs to know who acts (`the scheduler retries the job`). The rule
# sits at Optional, because naming the actor helps less often here than
# after a modal verb.
#
# Expression
#
# The rule matches a form of `be` followed by a participle (`retried`,
# `written`). A participle is the `-ing` or `-ed` form of a verb, such
# as `returning` or `cached`. `passive` lists three forms after a form
# of `be`: a verb form, a `by` form, and an agent form. `bare` lists the
# same three forms after the word `be`.
#
# The verb form is a verb, with or without an adverb or `not` before it.
# An adverb is a word that describes a verb, an adjective, or a whole
# sentence, such as `safely` or `also`. Harper's part-of-speech tagger
# marks each word with a tag for its word class. The filter
# `<VERB, !@stative>` requires one token to carry the tag `VERB` (a
# verb) and not to be one of the stative words. Stative words name a
# state instead of an action, and the tagger also marks them as verbs.
# `not` sits beside the adverb slot, because the tagger does not mark it
# as one (`the value is not written`).
#
# The tagger marks many participles as adjectives (`cached`, `retried`,
# `flushed`), so `missed` lists the common ones by hand. The `!-` glued
# after the verb skips a hyphenated compound whose first half the tagger
# marks as a verb (`is read-only`, `are fail-open`). The guard takes a
# punctuation mark too, so `the file is skipped.` still counts.
#
# The `by` form is three words in a row: a word that the rule reads as
# the participle, `by`, and the word that opens the agent. It exists
# because the tagger misses many participles (`is guarded by a lock`).
# The filter on the first word rejects every stative word. It also
# rejects every word that the tagger marks as an adjective, an adverb, a
# noun, a preposition, a determiner, a pronoun, or an exclamation. A
# preposition is a word that ties a noun to the rest of the sentence,
# such as `of`, `with`, or `by`. A determiner is a word that introduces
# a noun, such as `the`, `a`, or `this`.
#
# Weir has no token class for a number, so the word after `by` must be a
# pronoun, a proper noun, or a determiner instead. A proper noun is a
# name that takes a capital letter, such as `Kubernetes`. `by a lock`,
# `by the scheduler`, and `by git` count as agents.
#
# The agent form is a stative word, `by`, and an agent. Stative words
# still read as a passive when an agent follows them (`the flag is set
# by the caller`, `the binary is built by the pipeline`), so `agentive`
# lists the stative words that take one. The agent must start with a
# determiner, a pronoun, or a proper noun, as in the `by` form, and the
# determiner must not open `by the time` or `by the way`.
#
# `main` has three branches. The first takes `passive`, and it counts
# only when the token glued before the form of `be` is not a period. The
# second takes `bare`, and it counts only when the token before `be` is
# not a modal verb, `not`, or an adverb (`can also be configured`). Weir
# has no lookbehind, so each filter consumes the token before the form
# of `be` and narrows the finding back to the passive. The third takes
# `being` and any participle but `said`. The verb form in `passive`
# takes every other form of `be` through the filter `<@be, !being>`.
#
# A sentence that opens with a form of `be` is usually a question. A
# list item that continues its lead-in also opens with one (`- are
# created automatically`), and the first branch misses that passive.
# Four such items turned up in about 900 human-written docs. A Go commit
# message names a method such as `errors.Is` more often, so the first
# branch keeps its period filter.
#
# False Positives
#
# The stative list holds a progressive, a form of `be` plus an `-ing`
# verb (`has been running` is not a passive, because its subject acts).
# It also holds the participles that docs use as adjectives (`the flag
# is set`, `is enabled by default`, `the port is stuck`) and the naming
# idioms (`is called name`, `is known as a handle`, `is meant for
# testing`). `live` is stative because the tagger marks it as a verb (`a
# caller whose context is live`). `exported` and `deprecated` stay out
# of `missed`, because a Go doc uses each to name a state (`the field is
# exported`, `the method is deprecated`).
#
# A `by` that names no actor passes. The filter on the first word of the
# `by` form skips a degree (`off by one`) and a deadline (`empty by the
# time`). The limit on the word after `by` skips a dimension (`64 by
# 64`), a criterion (`keyed by path`), and the phrases `by default`, `by
# design`, and `by hand`.
#
# Forms of `get` stay out on purpose. Matching ignores case, so a
# `get VERB` branch fires on `Get returns the value` in a Go doc
# comment. The tagger also rarely marks the participle after `gets`
# (`gets flushed` goes unflagged either way).
#
# Matching ignores case, so a Go method named `Is` also reads as the
# verb (`errors.Is matches when any error in the tree matches`). The
# period filter in `main` skips it.
#
# Two forms stay flagged on purpose. One is an identifier used as a noun
# after `is` (`The default is print`), which belongs in a code span. The
# other is a participle that names the state an event leaves behind
# (`The issue is closed`). The tagger cannot tell it from the event, and
# the prose skill rewrites it anyway.
#
# Related Rules
#
# The Recommended ProsePassive reports a passive after a modal verb
# (`the timeout can be configured`), and this tier reports every other
# passive. The filter on `bare` skips the word `be` after a modal verb,
# `not`, or an adverb, so the two tiers never report the same span. A
# passive after `to` stays in this tier (`to be configured`).
#
# ProseFutureTense reports the `will` in `will be` plus a participle.
# `modal` holds `will`, so this tier skips that passive.
#
# ProseIntent reports the purpose wrappers (`is designed to`, `is
# intended to`), so the stative list holds `designed` and `intended`,
# and each phrase draws one finding. `recommended`, `advised`, and
# `encouraged` are stative for the same reason, because the Recommended
# ProseObligation reports `is recommended` and `are encouraged to`.
#
# ProseConcessive reports the opener `That being said`, so the `being`
# branch skips `said`.
#
# Measurement
#
# The rule hit 6150 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 20 of 20 sampled
# hits were passives.
expr stative [PROG, missing, done, set, unset, enabled, disabled, required, needed, expected, allowed, supported, available, stuck, broken, gone, finished, lost, left, known, frozen, wedged, based, tied, built, made, fixed, live, called, named, meant, supposed, recommended, designed, intended, bound, referred, prepared, advised, assured, reminded, warned, encouraged]
expr missed [cached, compiled, computed, configured, decoded, discarded, encoded, evicted, fetched, flushed, hashed, linted, normalized, parsed, patched, persisted, propagated, queued, redirected, reset, restarted, retried, revoked, rotated, sanitized, serialized, stripped, synced, tagged, truncated, validated]
expr be [is, are, was, were, been, being, isn't, aren't, wasn't, weren't]
expr verb [<VERB, !@stative>, @missed]
expr agentive [set, unset, enabled, disabled, required, needed, expected, allowed, supported, broken, known, built, made, fixed, called, named, designed, intended, bound, prepared, advised, assured, reminded, warned]
expr agent [(DET ![time, way]), PRON, PROPN]
expr passive [(<@be, !being> [@verb, ([ADV, not] @verb)]!-), (@be <!@stative, !ADJ, !ADV, !NOUN, !ADP, !DET, !PRON, !INTJ> by [DET, PRON, PROPN]), (@be [@agentive, ([ADV, not] @agentive)] by @agent)]
expr modal [must, should, can, cannot, could, may, might, will, would, shall, can't, won't, shouldn't, couldn't, wouldn't, mustn't]
expr bare [(be [@verb, ([ADV, not] @verb)]!-), (be <!@stative, !ADJ, !ADV, !NOUN, !ADP, !DET, !PRON, !INTJ> by [DET, PRON, PROPN]), (be [@agentive, ([ADV, not] @agentive)] by @agent)]
expr main [<(!(.)@passive), @passive>, <(![@modal, not, ADV] @bare), @bare>, (being [<@verb, !said>, ([ADV, not] @verb)]!-)]
let message "Passive voice. Name the actor when the reader needs to know who acts."
let description "Flags a passive without a modal verb before it, such as `the job is retried` or `the map is guarded by a lock`."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
allows "The scheduler retries the job."
allows "The scheduler is retrying the job."
allows "The job has been running for an hour."
allows "She is tired."
allows "The lock is free."
allows "There is a lock."
allows "Get returns the value stored under key."
allows "The flag is enabled by default."
allows "When the flag is set, the hook skips the file."
allows "The file is missing."
allows "The count is off by one."
allows "The queue is empty by the time the loop runs."
allows "The image is 64 by 64 pixels."
allows "The two panes are side by side."
allows "The output is stable by design."
allows "The mapping is done by hand."
allows "The migration is done by Friday."
allows "The field is called name."
allows "The type is known as a handle."
allows "The flag is meant for testing."
allows "The hook is designed to run after every write."
allows "It is recommended that you pin the version."
allows "The field is exported."
allows "The method is deprecated."
allows "The port is stuck in CLOSE_WAIT."
allows "Be warned that the flag is sticky."
allows "The default is lookup by id."
allows "The answer is yes by the way."
allows "The flag is set by the time the hook runs."
allows "The value is set by hand."
allows "The lock is read-only while the sweep runs."
allows "Reads are fail-open and writes are fail-closed."
allows "That being said, the hook skips the file."
allows "The errors.Is call matches when any error in the tree matches."
allows "A caller whose context is live loads again."
allows "The timeout can be configured."
allows "The flag can also be configured."
allows "The lock must not be copied."
allows "Users are encouraged to pin the version."
